WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:10.120
Emotional intelligence, also known as EI or&nbsp;
EQ, is the brain's ability to see feelings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:18.200
coming — and then to use them, listen to what&nbsp;
they’ve got to say, and manage them effectively.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:24.280
People with lots of EI recognize their own&nbsp;
emotions and can identify them in others. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:32.800
allows them to discern between different feelings,&nbsp;
adjust their inner states and communicate better.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.320
Perhaps the most relevant model&nbsp;
of “emotional intelligence” was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.440
developed by the psychologists John Mayer and&nbsp;
Peter Salovey, who defined it in 1990 as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:51.400
sum of four skills: perceiving, using,&nbsp;
understanding, and managing emotions.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:56.160
Perceiving Emotions is our ability&nbsp;
to detect them in faces, pictures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.880
and voices — including in ourselves. It’s&nbsp;
the foundation of emotional intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:08.240
and makes all other processing of&nbsp;
sentimental information possible.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.280
Using emotions is the ability to align&nbsp;
our hearts with our heads. People who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.240
are emotionally intelligent can perceive&nbsp;
their changing moods and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.800
may change what they do to channel&nbsp;
their feelings effectively.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.240
Understanding emotions is our ability&nbsp;
to comprehend the complex relationships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:38.240
between feelings. It involves recognizing and&nbsp;
describing how moods evolve and change over time.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.760
Managing emotions is the ability to&nbsp;
regulate feelings in ourselves and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.960
in others. People who are good at&nbsp;
that can control their sentiments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.520
manage their moods, and are able to&nbsp;
influence the emotions of others.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.520
While some people seem to be naturally gifted with&nbsp;
all four abilities, others have to work hard on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:06.240
every single one of them. The good news is, most&nbsp;
of us can improve our EI through mindfulness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:13.600
exercises, therapy, and education.&nbsp;
And it seems it’s worth the effort!

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.160
Higher emotional intelligence often&nbsp;
correlates with higher academic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.240
professional success. This is&nbsp;
because our emotions and the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:29.880
we manage them impact directly on our&nbsp;
brain’s ability to listen and learn.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.280
As Peter Salovey concluded: “People in good moods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.400
are better at inductive reasoning&nbsp;
and creative problem-solving.”

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.520
So what do you think? Is emotional intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.080
something we can and should try&nbsp;
to teach in schools? And if so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:59.760
how? Tell us your thoughts and experiences of&nbsp;
improving your own EI in the comments below.

